power: supply: bq2429x: don't clear the fault register from sysfs

REG09 (fault status) is read-to-clear: the first read after a fault
returns the latched fault, the next one the current state.  The driver
reads it in bq2429x_usb_detect(), under var_lock, keeps the value in
di->r9, and acts on it (CHRG_FAULT drops the input, NTC_FAULT feeds
the health property).

show_registers() reads registers 0 to 10 over i2c with no lock and
throws the values away.  A read of the "registers" file that lands
between a fault and the driver's next read of REG09 clears the latch,
so the driver never sees that fault and never logs it.  A monitor that
reads the file once a second hits this window sooner or later, and the
faults it hides (a hot battery while charging) are the ones it is
watching for.

Print the driver's cached r9 for REG09 instead of reading the chip,
and hold var_lock for the whole dump so it can't interleave with
bq2429x_usb_detect().  The other registers are still read live; none
of them change when read.

The value shown for REG09 is now the one from the driver's last read,
so it is 00 until the driver has read the register once.
---
--- a/drivers/power/supply/bq2429x_charger.c
+++ b/drivers/power/supply/bq2429x_charger.c
@@ -375,15 +375,25 @@
 	int len = 0;
 	int i;
 
+	mutex_lock(&di->var_lock);
 	for (i = 0; i < 11; i++) {
 		int n;
 
-		bq2429x_read(di->client, i, &buffer, 1);
+		/*
+		 * REG09 latches faults until it is read, so reading it here
+		 * would clear a fault before bq2429x_usb_detect() sees it.
+		 * Show the value the driver read last instead.
+		 */
+		if (i == FAULT_STATS_REGISTER)
+			buffer = di->r9;
+		else
+			bq2429x_read(di->client, i, &buffer, 1);
 		n = scnprintf(buf, 256, "reg %02x value %02x\n",
 			      i, buffer);
 		buf += n;
 		len += n;
 	}
+	mutex_unlock(&di->var_lock);
 	return len;
 }
 
